M<sup>3</sup> is an [[imaging spectrometer]] that provided the first high-resolution spatial and spectral map of the entire lunar surface, revealing the minerals of which it is made. This information will both provide clues to the early development of the [[Solar System]] and guide future [[astronauts]] to stores of precious resources.

This instrument is a [[Discovery Program]] "Mission of Opportunity" (a NASA-designed instrument on board another space agency's spacecraft).

Chandrayaan-1 operated for 312 days as opposed to the intended two years but the mission achieved many of its planned objectives.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://timesofindia.indiatimes.com/india/india-likely-to-land-on-moon-on-september-6/articleshow/69136567.cms|title=India likely to land on Moon on September 6|date=May 2, 2019|access-date=January 12, 2023|work=The Times of India|quote=Chandrayaan operated for 312 days as opposed to the intended two years but the mission achieved 95% of its planned objectives}}</ref> M<sup>3</sup> was used to map over 95% of the lunar surface in its low-resolution Global mode, but only a small fraction in the high-resolution Target mode.<ref>{{cite web|last=Boardman|first=Joe|title=A New Lunar Globe as Seen by the Moon Mineralogy Mapper: Image Coverage, Spectral Dimensionality and Statistical Anomalies.|url=https://www.planetary.brown.edu/pdfs/m3_1716.pdf|publisher=LPI|accessdate=12 April 2011|archive-date=16 July 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190716224623/http://www.planetary.brown.edu/pdfs/m3_1716.pdf|url-status=dead}}</ref> After suffering from several technical issues including failure of the star sensors and poor thermal shielding, Chandrayaan-1 stopped sending radio signals at 1:30 AM IST on 29 August 2009 shortly after which, the ISRO officially declared the mission over.

=== HVM<sup>3</sup> ===
The design of the M<sup>3</sup> instrument was used as the basis for the HVM<sup>3</sup> instrument on the unsuccessful [[Lunar Trailblazer]] mission. HVM<sup>3</sup> was a [[Pushbroom scanner|pushbroom]] [[Infrared#Commonly used subdivision scheme|short-wave infrared]] imaging spectrometer.<ref>{{Cite web | url=https://www.hou.usra.edu/meetings/lpsc2022/pdf/2316.pdf | title=Lunar trailblazer: a pioneering smallsat for lunar water and lunar geology | website=www.hou.usra.edu}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last1=Klima |first1=Rachel |last2=Pieters |first2=Carle |last3=Green |first3=Robert |last4=Blaney |first4=Diana |last5=Ehlmann |first5=Bethany |last6=Thompson |first6=David |last7=Bowles |first7=Neil |last8=Calcutt |first8=Simon |last9=Donaldson Hanna |first9=Kerri |date=January 2021 |title=Directly Characterizing Surficial Hydroxyl/Water on the Moon with the Lunar Trailblazer Mission |url=https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2021cosp...43E.352K/abstract |journal=43rd COSPAR Scientific Assembly. Held 28 January - 4 February |language=en |volume=43 |pages=352 |bibcode=2021cosp...43E.352K }}</ref> The Lunar Trailblazer spacecraft was launched on 27 February 2025, but NASA lost contact with the spacecraft.<ref>{{Cite web |date=2025-08-04 |title=NASA's Lunar Trailblazer Moon Mission Ends - NASA |url=https://www.nasa.gov/missions/small-satellite-missions/lunar-trailblazer/nasas-lunar-trailblazer-moon-mission-ends/ |access-date=2025-09-07 |language=en-US}}</ref> The mission ended on July 31, 2025 after all attempts to contact the spacecraft were unsuccessful.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Berger |first=Eric |date=2025-03-05 |title=NASA just lost yet another one of its low-cost planetary missions |url=https://arstechnica.com/space/2025/03/nasa-just-lost-yet-another-one-of-its-low-cost-planetary-missions/ |access-date=2025-09-07 |website=Ars Technica |language=en}}</ref>{{-}}

